  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This Figshare-hosted dataset provides the supplementary tables for a 2024 study on high-capacity sample multiplexing in single-cell chromatin accessibility profiling. The clearest evidence of impact is reuse: the record shows one documented downstream reuse by the associated article, while citation and public attention signals remain at zero in the available indexes.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published as an open dataset in Figshare and linked to the journal article in <em>BMC Genomics</em>, this resource serves as supporting data for the sciPlex-ATAC3 workflow described by Booth and colleagues. The dataset centers on supplementary tables that summarize differential accessibility patterns and recovered nuclei statistics across conditions, making it a methodological companion rather than a standalone narrative article.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest evidence of use is internal to the scholarly record: the reuse graph identifies one downstream reuse, and that reuse is the associated article itself. This indicates that the dataset is already integrated into the publication it supports, which is a meaningful form of documented scholarly use for a supplementary data release.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At the same time, the broader impact record is still sparse. OpenAlex reports zero citations, and the available scite and event-data signals also show no recorded mentions or citation classifications. DataCite similarly shows no views or downloads in the captured record, so the current picture is one of early-stage or lightly indexed circulation rather than wider measured uptake.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Stewardship signals are straightforward and positive: the dataset is openly available through Figshare, assigned a DOI, and registered as an open dataset in repository registries. The author list links the work primarily to the University of Washington, with Howard Hughes Medical Institute also represented among the contributors, underscoring its connection to an established stem-cell and genomics research environment.</p>",
